          "text": "Now I have a good picture. Let me check a couple more things:\n- How `parse_point` (C) is declared vs C++ — signatures differ. C: `parse_point(const char* str, struct Point* p)` returns int. C++: `parse_point(std::string_view)` returns expected.\n- The C tests are currently .c files linked to *_c_static. The user wants the C-side tests to be gtest, written as .cpp that include the C header. But the C header geometry.h has no `extern \"C\"`. So we need to add it.\n\nLet me understand what the user precisely said: \"мне нужно перенести юнит тесты Си-часть на этот фреймворк (extern \"C\" в geometry.h/string_utils.h (правка публичных заголовков, для C-потребителей инертна), тесты — .cpp с C-заголовком, линк к *_c_static, те же имена целей).\"\n\nSo the plan:\n1. Add `extern \"C\"` guards to geometry.h / string_utils.h (and parse_codes.h? It only defines an enum - enum constants in C++ have internal linkage / are constants, not symbols, so no extern \"C\" needed, but including it from a .cpp is fine). Actually parse_codes.h defines an unnamed enum with constants; in C++ that's fine. No functions. So no extern \"C\" needed. But need it for the headers with function declarations.\n\n2. Convert the C tests from .c to .cpp, including gtest, using the C API (e.g. `parse_point(in, &p)` returning int, `struct Point`).\n\n3. Update tests/unit/CMakeLists.txt: rename targets? \"те же имена целей\" - the user says \"те же имена целей\" (same target names). Hmm. That likely means keep the target names like `parse_point_c` etc. But wait - if both parse_point_c (gtest) and parse_point_cpp (gtest) exist... Actually the current naming: `parse_point_c` (C) and `parse_point_cpp` (C++). So keep `parse_point_c` as target name, but now it's a gtest exe built from parse_point.cpp (C test written in C++).\n\nWait, but then the file name would be parse_point.c → parse_point.cpp? But there's already parse_point.cpp (C++ side test). That's a conflict! Both can't be parse_point.cpp in the same directory.\n\nHmm. Let me re-read: \"тесты — .cpp с C-заголовком, линк к *_c_static, те же имена целей\". So the C tests become .cpp files, include the C header, link to *_c_static, and keep the same target names (`parse_point_c`, etc.).\n\nBut file naming collision: `parse_point.cpp` already exists for C++. So the C test file must have a different filename. Options: `parse_point_c.cpp`? Or put under a different dir? The user says \"те же имена целей\" (same target names) but doesn't specify file names. So likely the C test source files get renamed to something like `parse_point_c.cpp`.\n\nHmm, but that's a naming decision requiring the user's input. Actually, maybe the structure is: there's a directory per side? Let me check the actual file list to see if there's already a naming convention.\n\nLet me glob tests/unit.\n\nActually wait — maybe the intent is different. Currently the C tests are the \"references\"/manual checkers. On the C++ side they migrated to gtest as `parse_point.cpp` (target parse_point_cpp). Now migrating the C side to gtest: the C test would be `parse_point_c.cpp`? Or maybe they'd keep the .c file name but compile as C++? No.\n\nLet me look at what naming makes sense and check git or other clues. Let me glob the directory.\n\nAlso, I should verify: does `#include \"../../common/geometry.h\"` from a .cpp work with the enum `NUMBER_OK` etc.? parse_codes.h has unnamed enum, fine.\n\nAlso crucial: the C header uses `struct Point` and `struct` keyword. In C++ you can write `Point` without struct, but `struct Point` also works. Fine.\n\nAnother important issue: the test data uses C strings; gtest .cpp can call the C API which takes `const char*`. Good.\n\nBut there's a subtlety: the C-side gtest tests can't use `parse_point(std::string_view)`; they call `parse_point(in, &p)`. So the test bodies differ from C++ versions.\n\nLet me check file listing and the README / AGENTS for the layer 19 plan details. Also check if there's a plan doc. Let me glob tests/unit and look at README's unit section.",
