or. So C-side gtest tests should have a separate printer for the C Point, or use PrintToString with default (byte dump) - bad.\n\nOptions:\n1. C-side tests include only the C header, and define their own `PrintTo(const struct Point&, ...)` in a separate header (e.g. `printers_c.hpp`) or inline in each .cpp.\n2. Or, C-side tests use `EXPECT_NEAR` on individual fields and print custom operator<< for their cases.\n3. Or simply not use PrintTo for Point and instead compare fields with EXPECT_NEAR and print via a custom operator<<.\n\nActually, there's a deeper issue: if the C-side test .cpp includes geometry.h which declares `struct Point` and also includes printers.hpp which includes geometry.hpp which defines `struct Point { ... operator== }` — redefinition. So they must be kept separate. A separate `printers_c.hpp` that includes geometry.h is the clean solution. But wait, would defining `PrintTo(const struct Point&...)` in the global namespace work with the C struct? Yes.\n\nHmm, but is name collision an issue at all if geometry.h is guarded with `extern \"C\"`? The guard is only around declarations; the struct definition is outside. But both headers define `struct Point` in global scope; double inclusion → error. Confirmed [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]. Actually, let me reconsider: maybe geometry.h wrapped in extern \"C\" would put the struct inside extern \"C\"? No — you wouldn't wrap the struct definition in extern \"C\" (well, you can, C linkage applies to functions and variables, structs are unaffected but allowed inside extern \"C\" block). Regardless, having identical struct definition twice is still an ODR violation / redefinition error. Actually, if the definitions are token-identical, it's... no, you cannot redefine a class in the same TU even identically. So must avoid.\n\nSo C-side tests need their own printer header. Good catch. Let me note it.\n\nNow about the `is_hex_prefix` test: there's no C++ counterpart, only C. It has only a C-side test. So we keep target `is_h