C++ counterpart) → 10 if we count... wait: parse_point, parse_int32, is_empty, is_hex_prefix, trim_string, point_distance, centroid, polygon_vertex, parse_radius, vertex_form_index = 10 targets. Yes, 10 C targets. C++ has 9 (no is_hex_prefix).\n\nNow the CMakeLists changes: replace each C `add_executable(... .c)` block with `add_unit_gtest(<name>_c <name>_c.cpp <lib>_c_static)`. Note: is_hex_prefix_c also uses add_unit_gtest.\n\nBut add_unit_gtest links `GTest::gtest_main` and calls `deploy_vcpkg_dlls`. The C-side tests link to `*_c_static`; on Windows `COMMON_STATIC` is PUBLIC on those libs, so `__declspec(dllimport)` avoided. Fine.\n\nWait — a subtlety with `add_unit_gtest` and `configure_target(${target} ON)`: for the C test compiled as C++ (since .cpp), `$<$<COMPILE_LANGUAGE:CXX>:/EHsc>` applies. Good.\n\nAnother subtlety: The C static libs (`string_utils_c_static`, `common_c_static`) compile .c as C. Fine.\n\nNow, `extern \"C\"` in the C headers. Important detail: The header `geometry.h` also has `#include \"parse_codes.h\"`. In a .cpp, `parse_codes.h`'s enum is fine. But should `extern \"C\"` wrap only the function declarations? Typically you write:\n\n```c\n#ifdef __cplusplus\nextern \"C\" {\n#endif\n...\n#ifdef __cplusplus\n}\n#endif\n```\n\nWrapping everything including the struct is fine (structs aren't affected by language linkage). Simplest: wrap the whole body. But the `COMMON_API` macro and includes... Typically you put the guard after includes, around declarations. The struct Point definition can be inside or outside; safest to put the functions inside, and struct can be either. Many projects wrap the whole thing.\n\nBut note: `geometry.h` includes `<parse_codes.h>` and `string_utils.h` includes `<stdbool.h>`, `<stdint.h>`. These should be outside the extern \"C\" block (system headers already have their own guards, but wrapping them is harmless-ish; better outside).\n\nI'll recommend the standard pattern.\n\nAlso, does `string_utils.h` include geomet