pename Pt>\ninline void PrintTo(const Pt& p, std::ostream* os) {\n    *os << std::format(\"({:.3f}, {:.3f}, {:.3f})\", p.x, p.y, p.z);\n}\n```\nThen both C and C++ tests include the same `printers.hpp`, no geometry include. This is elegant and DRY. Risk: a function template `PrintTo` visible in global namespace might be selected for unrelated types. But gtest calls `PrintTo(value, os)` via ADL/unqualified; a template in global namespace is found by ordinary unqualified lookup only if it's declared before the point of use in the TU. Since printers.hpp is included at top of test file, yes it's visible. For a type like std::string, the template would instantiate with std::string having no .x → hard error, not SFINAE, which could break compilation of EXPECT_EQ on strings in that TU. Hmm, that's a real risk: e.g., a test file includes printers.hpp and does EXPECT_EQ on std::string; gtest's universal printer might try PrintTo(std::string, os) and pick our template → substitution failure? It's not SFINAE-friendly (failure in body, not signature) → hard error. Actually gtest's printer for std::string is more specialized/overloaded. gtest's `PrintTo` overloads for std::string exist in testing namespace and are found via ADL? std::string's associated namespace is std, which has `std::to_string` but no PrintTo. gtest provides `PrintTo(const std::string&, ostream*)` in `testing` namespace. Unqualified call with ADL looks in testing (because gtest internals call from testing namespace) — so testing::PrintTo(std::string) is found, plus our global template. Overload resolution: non-template exact match vs template exact match → non-template preferred. So gtest's wins. Probably OK.\n\nStill, a non-template `printers_c.hpp` is the zero-risk option. Given the user's careful style, I'd recommend `printers_c.hpp` for the C struct. It's one small file, clear. And keeps `printers.hpp` untouched.\n\nHmm, but actually — do the C tests need the Point printer at all? Only the ones with Point