e `<cstdbool>`/`<cstdint>` equivalents; including `<stdbool.h>` and `<stdint.h>` from C++ is fine (they're C headers, provide the names in global namespace). `<stdbool.h>` in C++ defines `__bool_true_false_are_defined`? It's fine; `bool` builtin. [ФАКТ, ВЫСОКИЙ]\n\nNow, potential issue: `string_utils.h` includes `<stdbool.h>` which in C++ is deprecated but works. Fine.\n\nLet me write it.\n\nActually, I want to double-check one thing about `extern \"C\"` and the dllexport/dllimport with the macro. The macro is defined before the extern \"C\" block. If we place extern \"C\" around functions only, the `COMMON_API` on each function stays appropriate. Good.\n\nAlso, an alternative to editing headers: define the `extern \"C\"` in the test .cpp AND keep headers untouched. But user explicitly wants header edit. And header edit is the correct, reusable solution (any future C++ consumer gets correct linkage). I agree with the user here.\n\nLet me now write the plan. Keep it structured with headings. Then ask questions.\n\nLet me count implementation files: \n- Edit 2 headers.\n- Add 10 .cpp test files.\n- Add 1 printers_c.hpp.\n- Rewrite tests/unit/CMakeLists.txt (10 blocks → 10 add_unit_gtest lines).\n- Delete 10 .c files.\n- (optional) README/CHANGELOG/AGENTS.\n\nThat's the plan.\n\nWait, the user said \"те же имена целей\" — same target names. My plan keeps `parse_point_c` etc. Good. But note: currently the C targets are plain `add_test(NAME parse_point_c COMMAND parse_point_c)` — one test named `parse_point_c`. After migration it becomes discovered subtests. Target name same, test names change (with prefix). I'll flag this.\n\nNow the questions. I'll ask:\nQ1: C test source file naming/location.\nOptions: \n- `tests/unit/<name>_c.cpp` (recommended)\n- `tests/unit/c/<name>.cpp` (keep basename, split dirs)\n- other\n\nQ2: Pretty-printing the C Point in failure messages.\nOptions:\n- separate `tests/unit/printers_c.hpp` with PrintTo for `struct Point` (recommended)\n- reuse