          "type": "reasoning",
          "text": "The user asks: \"кратко напомни. в негатив. тестах мы меняем именно want верно?\" — In negative-control tests, we change exactly `want`, right?\n\nLet me answer briefly and accurately. The purpose of negative control (негативный контроль) is to verify the test actually FAILS when the expected value is wrong — i.e., prove the test detects a real mismatch, not that it's vacuous/vacuous-passing.\n\nTwo things to correct/clarify:\n1. In negative control we tamper with the **expected value** (the test's `want`), so the test should fail. Yes, typically change `want` in the Case data (or an expected code).\n2. But it's not only `want`. The journal shows both:\n   - changing `want` (expected) — e.g., `OkCase{\"1 2 3\", {1,2,4}}`;\n   - changing the **error code** in `ErrCase` (also `want`) — e.g., `number_error::not_number → too_much`;\n   - changing actual code (`got` side / production logic) — the journal mentions for parse_int32 the AI \"советует делать изменение именно в коде ошибки а не во входных данных\" — hmm, that's about which side.\n\nActually the key point: negative control = introduce a deliberate mismatch between `got` and `want`, then check the test fails. You can do this by either:\n- altering `want` (test expectation), or\n- altering the production code / input so `got` differs.\n\nThe user's routine in the journal mostly altered `want` (expected data) — e.g., \"OkCase{\"1 2 3\", Point{1,2,4}}\", \"centroid want {5,-2,3}->{5,-2,1}\", \"vertex_form_index check(1,0)->(1,1)\", \"parse_radius check_ok(\"5\",5.0)->5.5\". All these change the expectation (`want`). Also for ErrCase they changed the expected error code (which is the \"want\" of that suite).\n\nBut note: for the negative control to be meaningful, the point is to prove the test *can* fail — that it isn't silently passing. Changing `want` is the cleanest: it doesn't touch production code (no risk of leaving a bug), and it directly exercises the `EXPECT`/`ASSERT` comparison.\n\nAlso the journal says: \"deepseek советует делать изменение именно в коде ошибки а не во входных данных\" for parse_int32 ErrCase — meaning change the expected error code (want) rather than the input, so it's the got/want mismatch that fails rather than a different input path. So yes — change `want`.\n\nAlso there was one case of changing the source under test (e.g., `point_distance` changing result? \"point_distance где нулевое расстояние, меняю его на 0.1\" — that changed the expected want 0.0→0.1, not the code). And \"polygon_vertex check(0,4,1,0)->check(0,4,1,1)\" — want_x 0→1. So all want.\n\nOne case: negative control on `string_utils` EOF defect was not a negative control per se. And for `version.h.in` they changed `@PROJECT_VERSION@` to 0.9.9 — that changes the program output (got), not the test want. Hmm, that's a negative control of a different form: changed the source of truth so the program output diverges from the generated expectation. So not always `want`.\n\nSo: the dominant pattern is changing `want`, yes. But strictly, negative control = force got≠want; you can do it from either side. Changing `want` is preferred because it doesn't risk leaving production changes.\n\nLet me answer concisely: yes, mostly want; explain why; note the alternative and the nuance.\n\nKeep it brief. Plan mode — no edits.\n\nLet me also note: after negative control, revert the change and rerun to green. And record command+output.\n\nAnswer in Russian, brief.",
